# Commit message rules

## Subject prefix (required)

Start the subject line with one of these prefixes in square brackets:

- **[Feature]** — New functionality or user-facing capability
- **[Task]** — General work items, improvements, or non-bug changes
- **[Bug]** — Bug fixes
- **[Docs]** — Documentation only (README, comments, guides)
- **[Refactor]** — Code structure or cleanup without changing behavior
- **[Chore]** — Tooling, dependencies, config, scripts, housekeeping
- **[Test]** — Adding or updating tests only
- **[Config]** — Build settings, entitlements, xcconfig, certificates

## Format

- **Word limit (subject):** Maximum 30 words; never exceed. Preferred length is around 15 words.
- Use **past tense**: "Changed" not "Change", "Added" not "Add", "Fixed" not "Fix".
- One subject line; no period at the end.
- Optional body after a blank line for more context or "why".
- Reference issues/tickets when relevant (e.g. "Fixes #123").
- **Personality (optional, sparing):** Occasionally add a very short, light sign-off at the end of the message (e.g. "What a session!!", "This was looong!!"). Do not do this for every commit — only when it genuinely fits (e.g. after a big or satisfying change). Keep it brief and rare.

## Examples

- `[Feature] Added email sign-in to auth flow`
- `[Bug] Fixed crash when profile image was nil`
- `[Task] Moved rules into .cursor/rules/`
- `[Docs] Updated Firebase setup steps`
- `[Refactor] Extracted validation into separate type`
- `[Chore] Bumped Firebase SDK to 10.22.1`

With optional personality (use sparingly):

- `[Feature] Added email sign-in to auth flow. What a session!!`
- `[Task] Moved rules into .cursor/rules/. This was looong!!`
